{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-72328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100x}{{x}^{2}-1}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-72328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100x}{{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[100x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100}{2x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-72328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "285dfc88d1b41ab74575d2f461b238022ea779501eee859121d3b18f9acb4002"} {"question" "q-72328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-72328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\infty "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-72328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-114245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-114245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If we plug in $x=1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{{[2x-2]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-114245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "285dfc88d1b41ab74575d2f461b238022ea779501eee859121d3b18f9acb4002"} {"question" "q-114245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-114245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-114245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-114249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-114249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If we plug in $x=-1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{[(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1]}^{′}}{{[{x}^{2}+2x+1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{2x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)]}^{′}}{{[2x+2]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x+2})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{2(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-114249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "285dfc88d1b41ab74575d2f461b238022ea779501eee859121d3b18f9acb4002"} {"question" "q-114249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-114249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-114249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-115526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-115526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{-{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003623/state.json" "file_sha256" "dcc2cc3c0ee7093295e140b6bc889389949e58d35c28142edc07667c67fb0e18"} {"question" "q-115526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Develope